  "explanation": "The core greedy strategy implemented is incorrect. The problem asks to maximize the length of the LNDS by choosing values `a_i` within `[l_i, r_i]`. The implemented logic of removing the smallest `r` from `active_rs` when `active_rs.lower_bound(l)` is not `begin()` does not correctly model the problem. The `active_rs` should represent the smallest possible ending values of non-decreasing subsequences of certain lengths, not just a collection of `r_i` values. The condition for a valid non-decreasing subsequence is `a_{i_1} <= a_{i_2} <= ... <= a_{i_m}`, where `a_{i_j} \u2208 [l_{i_j}, r_{i_j}]`. This implies that for any two consecutive elements `a_x, a_y` in the subsequence, `l_x \u2264 a_x \u2264 r_x` and `l_y \u2264 a_y \u2264 r_y`, and `a_x \u2264 a_y`. This means we must have `l_x \u2264 r_y`. The current logic does not correctly capture this interaction to maximize the LNDS length.",
